5255 18.128) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-2f\" d=\"M 269 4678 Q 269 4710 285 4729 Q 301 4749 346 4749 L 3789 4749 Q 3827 4749 3846 4729 Q 3866 4710 3866 4678 L 3866 4346 Q 3866 4282 3789 4282 L 2490 4282 Q 2419 4282 2419 4218 L 2419 83 Q 2419 0 2342 0 L 1882 0 Q 1805 0 1805 83 L 1805 4211 Q 1805 4282 1741 4282 L 346 4282 Q 269 4282 269 4346 L 269 4678 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-4f\" d=\"M 582 0 Q 499 0 499 83 L 499 4666 Q 499 4749 570 4749 L 947 4749 Q 1018 4749 1018 4672 L 1018 2963 Q 1018 2931 1030 2924 Q 1043 2918 1075 2944 Q 1299 3117 1488 3232 Q 1677 3347 1859 3401 Q 2042 3456 2259 3456 Q 2758 3456 3020 3190 Q 3283 2925 3283 2483 L 3283 102 Q 3283 0 3187 0 L 2829 0 Q 2784 0 2765 22 Q 2746 45 2746 83 L 2746 2368 Q 2746 2682 2586 2858 Q 2426 3034 2080 3034 Q 1894 3034 1731 2973 Q 1568 2912 1401 2800 Q 1235 2688 1037 2515 L 1037 96 Q 1037 0 934 0 L 582 0 L 582 0 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-2f\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-4f\" transform=\"translate(64.5 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-50\" transform=\"translate(122.90625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-6a\" transform=\"translate(146.8125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-a5\" transform=\"translate(198.015625 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-75\" transform=\"translate(219.421875 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-48\" transform=\"translate(270.125 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-3b\" transform=\"translate(328.21875 0)\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-69\" transform=\"translate(383.015625 0)\"/></g></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pea76a63990\"><rect x=\"43.373\" y=\"27.296\" width=\"345.96\" height=\"138.6\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · Variable cost per m² ($)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Profit per m² fell from $2.90 to $2.40. Bridge:</p> <ul><li>Volume: +0.4M m² × $2.90 = <b>+$1.16M</b></li> <li>Variable cost: −$0.50 × 8.4M = <b>−$4.20M</b></li> <li>Fixed costs: <b>−$0.90M</b></li></ul> <p>Kiln energy is +$0.45 of the $0.50 rise per m², 90% of it, even though gas got cheaper.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> each m² now needs more energy. That points to how the kilns are used, not what energy costs.</p>","note":"Volume: 8.0M m² last year, 8.4M m² this year. Fixed costs: $18.0M last year, $18.9M this year."},{"q":"Every time a kiln line changes colour or size it burns about $1,800 of gas heating up and running off rejects. Otherwise energy costs $1.15 per m². What does that tell you about average run length?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Energy per m² = $1.15 + $1,800 ÷ run length.</p> <ul><li>Last year: $1.30 → $1,800 ÷ run = $0.15 → run ≈ <b>12,000 m²</b></li> <li>This year: $1.75 → $1,800 ÷ run = $0.60 → run ≈ <b>3,000 m²</b></li></ul> <p><b>So-what:</b> average runs are four times shorter. The custom orders fill the factory with changeovers: busy, but not productive.</p>"},{"q":"Selva could set a minimum run of 6,000 m² and batch custom orders weekly. What is that worth? How would you keep design customers?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>New energy per m² = $1.15 + $1,800 ÷ 6,000 = <b>$1.45</b>, saving $0.30 per m²: about <b>$2.52M a year</b>, before any lost orders. Profit would rise to about $3.8M.</p> <p>To keep designers:</p><ul><li><b>Small-run surcharge</b> priced at the changeover cost ($1,800 per run), so small orders pay their way</li> <li><b>Weekly custom slot</b>: collect orders and run them together</li><li><b>Stock a curated range</b> of popular \"custom\" colours</li></ul>","note":"Strong candidates price the surcharge from the changeover cost rather than inventing a number."}],"reco":"Profit fell from $5.2M to $1.26M because small custom orders cut average kiln runs from about 12,000 to 3,000 m², raising energy per m² by 35% even as gas got cheaper. I recommend a 6,000 m² minimum run with weekly batching of custom work, worth about $2.5M a year and lifting profit to about $3.8M, plus a small-run surcharge that covers the changeover cost and should close the rest of the gap to the $4M target. Risk: some designers go elsewhere, so offer a stocked custom range. Next step: re-plan two months of orders to confirm the run lengths are achievable.","great":["Finds that energy use, not energy price, is the issue","Back-solves run length from the cost formula","Prices the surcharge from the true changeover cost","Keeps the design customers rather than dropping them"],"id":24,"slug":"selva-ceramics"}